\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Corpus composition. Shares are of total bytes; duplicate counts are exact-sha1 matches dropped before writing.\relax }}{14}{table.caption.7}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces One frozen checkpoint (large, step 10{,}000) under the sampled protocol. The six draws span more than the largest effect the ladder was built to measure. The exhaustive replicates are marked pending because the run directory was lost with the first sandbox; their reduction is not claimed in this build.\relax }}{18}{table.caption.11}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Fixed-budget scores surviving on this checkout (100M tokens, 30\% mask). The source column is the evaluation protocol that produced the score: \emph {exhaustive} where a full-score file survived, \emph {sampled} where only a summary survived, and \emph {lost} where the run directory was reclaimed. Sampled and exhaustive rows are not comparable, and the mid/large cells were lost with the first sandbox; no U-shape is claimed from this table. The generalisation gap is held-out over train loss, both in bits/byte. Generated by \texttt {thesis/gen\_tables.py}.\relax }}{18}{table.caption.12}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces The large rung as its token budget grows. The corpus holds 1{,}633{,}580{,}647{} unique bytes at this build, so tokens beyond 100M are additional epochs, not additional data.\relax }}{19}{table.caption.13}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Transfer from byte-level pretraining to decision accuracy at a matched budget: 2,000 training rows, 500 test rows, six nominal epochs, identical order, steps, batch, optimiser, learning rate and training seed. Banking77 is the class-balanced 77-way draw (three seeds, 500 test rows each); AG News is the class-balanced four-way draw (three seeds, 500 unique test rows, 1,500 pooled decisions). The AG interval is the paired crossed-seed/item bootstrap and crosses zero; the Banking77 8,000-row arm in Table\nobreakspace {}\ref {tab:rows} is a different, row-scaling experiment and is not this matched contrast. The per-seed AG rows are the same six cells, shown so the pooled interval cannot hide a sign change. Generated from \texttt {results/transfer/*.json} and the recovered six-cell summary.\relax }}{19}{table.caption.14}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces 9 public tasks at three rungs under one pre-registered recipe: LR $10^{-4}$, exactly 3{,}000 optimizer steps, batch 32, 4{,}000 stratified training rows and 2{,}000 test rows where available (Financial PhraseBank has 1{,}264 train and 1{,}000 test), seed 0, encoder unfrozen, native context of each checkpoint (256 at 14.4M, 1{,}024 at 33.7M and 113.47M). The recipe is identical for every cell; there is no per-task learning rate, step count, row cap or prompt. Every Azurite cell is measured on the dual context-candidate path. The 113.47M column is a single new run from fresh weights over 1{,}134{,}658{,}560 tokens (step 69{,}255), evaluated on the same test rows as the smaller rungs. Cited cells are published or third-party figures, not reproduced. DAIR Emotion and XNLI at the two smaller rungs changed evaluation path in an earlier pass and their former candidate-only values are retired. MASSIVE intent's cited point is Laya's card, not a Jev number. No interval is shown here; 95\% Wilson intervals and exact paired McNemar tests are in \S \ref {sec:suite} and \texttt {results/suite\_large\_113m/COMPARISON.md}. These are cross-harness comparisons, not a head-to-head.\relax }}{24}{table.caption.15}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Every 113.47M cell with its 95\% Wilson interval, its gap against the cited point, and the exact paired McNemar test against the 14.4M cell on the same test rows. $K$ is the number of classes, $n$ the number of scored test rows. Verdict is the interval rule: \emph {above} and \emph {below} mean the Wilson interval excludes the cited point, \emph {tie} that it covers it. The last two columns compare the two Azurite rungs directly, not either against the citation. Read from \texttt {results/suite\_large\_113m/COMPARISON.json}; the generator recomputes every cell and refuses a mismatch.\relax }}{25}{table.caption.16}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces Scaling at matched tokens per parameter ($D/N = 10$) on the 1{,}633{,}580{,}647-token corpus. \emph {Exhaustive} is the non-overlapping full held-out pass (9{,}967{,}360 scored bytes); \emph {sampled} is 64 windows of 1{,}024 bytes at mask seed 1234, the configuration every rung was scored with. The large rung has only the sampled score: its exhaustive pass was not run, so no exhaustive value is extrapolated for it. The fitted exponents are least squares on $\qopname \relax o{log}\mathrm {bpb}$ against $\qopname \relax o{log}N$, over the rungs that carry each protocol. The 256-byte context of the tiny rung bounds the sequence it was trained on, not the number of parameters it has.\relax }}{26}{table.caption.17}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.8}{\ignorespaces Context length at matched training tokens (14.26M parameters, 142.6M tokens each). Isolates sequence length from optimisation budget.\relax }}{27}{table.caption.18}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.9}{\ignorespaces The candidate-set sweep at 113.47M. Each row is a separate fine-tune of the same pretrained checkpoint, at the suite's frozen recipe (4{,}000 stratified rows, 3{,}000 steps, batch 32, LR $10^{-4}$, seed 0), differing only in the number of candidates $|C|$ packed into every training and test row. Test rows are the same 2{,}000 rows at every width. A fixed model restricted to a sub-bank is not an experiment: with the gold always present, deleting candidates can only remove wrong argmaxes, so restricted accuracy is guaranteed to be at least the full-bank accuracy. Width is therefore trained, not simulated. Wilson intervals are 95\%; NLL is over the sampled candidate set and is not comparable across widths. Read from \texttt {results/e4\_candidate\_sweep/SUMMARY.json}; \texttt {scripts/e4\_summary.py} verifies every per-example record before writing it.\relax }}{28}{table.caption.19}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Decode versus score with everything else fixed: same encoder, same initialisation, same rows in the same order, same steps, batch, learning rate and seed. \emph {Score} evaluates every candidate in one pass and cannot emit an out-of-bank label; \emph {decode} fills the label's byte slots iteratively and is matched against the bank, so it can. Latency is batch-1 wall clock on CPU over the same test rows, and therefore a lower bound on what an autoregressive decoder of the same label length would cost. GPU seconds per 1,000 decisions come from $1000\times p_{50}$ and are CPU seconds here, so they overstate an accelerator. The last column is the GPU rate at which 1,000 local decisions cost what Jev charges for that task's billed payload at \$0.042/MTok; it is rate-free because no price for this machine is recorded anywhere in the repository. Read from \texttt {results/decode\_vs\_score/*.json}.\relax }}{34}{table.caption.22}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Banking77 77-way, the public task-level comparison with a third-party Jev run. \emph {Measured} cells come from \texttt {scripts/decode\_vs\_score.py} on this machine, over a class-balanced draw of all seventy-seven labels; \emph {cited} cells come from a third party's run of a hosted API \citep {ayautomate2026jev}. The score row is the 8{,}000-row fine-tune; the decode row is the 1{,}600-row arm, so the row budgets differ within this table as well as across systems. The two systems were never evaluated on the same rows or the same hardware, so a difference of a few points would prove nothing and a large difference would.\relax }}{35}{table.caption.24}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces The protocol-matched Banking77 comparison. All 3{,}080 official test rows, in the public run's order; the same 24 retrieved labelled examples per row, in the same order; gold labels from the same frozen prediction file. Azurite is not given the 77 category definitions, so its information budget is strictly smaller than Jev's. The primary arm was fixed at A1 with a $-2.0$ point non-inferiority margin before any forward pass. Wilson intervals are 95\%; McNemar is exact and two-sided; the paired bootstrap lower bound is the 5th percentile of 10{,}000 resamples, seed 0. Read from \texttt {results/jev\_matched/summary.json}; the pre-registration hash is \texttt {ee1b5bb51a7b} and the evaluator-only amendment is \texttt {73f3fdc46cc1}.\relax }}{37}{table.caption.25}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces The message-query head on all 3{,}080 official Banking77 rows, with the exact 24 public Jev examples per row and the same gold/Jev vector as the matched test. ``Examples'' is the mean number of complete lines that fit in the 1{,}020-byte context budget. The old head is the E4 mean-pool head; the query head replaces mean pooling with a masked attention pool queried by the customer message. \texttt {old\_a2c} is the head control under the same packing; \texttt {query\_a0} is the example control. The P0 bar is Jev's 92.40\%. Wilson intervals are 95\%; McNemar is exact and two-sided; the bootstrap lower bound is the 5th percentile of 10{,}000 paired resamples, seed 0. Read from \texttt {results/jev\_query/eval/summary.json}.\relax }}{41}{table.caption.28}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces The 5{,}120-byte query head on all 3{,}080 official Banking77 rows. Following amendment 1 to the pre-registration, the encoder starts from the 1{,}024-position 113.47M checkpoint and is fine-tuned jointly with the query head for 3{,}000{} steps at sequence 5{,}120 and batch 8{} (1.69{} GPU-hours); the separate masked-LM length adaptation was not run. The head uses the same 4{,}000 rows and the exact stored 24 BM25 example ids as the native-window head. ``Examples'' is the mean number of complete lines in the 5{,}118-byte context budget. The old head and its native 1{,}020-byte packing are unchanged controls, reused from the native-window evaluation with their per-example context hashes checked. The P0 bar is Jev's 92.40\%. Wilson intervals are 95\%; McNemar is exact and two-sided; the bootstrap lower bound is the 5th percentile of 10{,}000 paired resamples, seed 0. Read from \texttt {results/jev\_query\_5120/eval/summary.json}.\relax }}{42}{table.caption.29}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.6}{\ignorespaces Fine-tune rows at the Banking77 77-way interface, exhaustive scoring, same pretrained checkpoint, order, steps-per-example, batch, learning rate and seeds. The two columns differ only in how many distinct training rows the five epochs cover. Generated by \texttt {thesis/gen\_scaling.py}.\relax }}{46}{table.caption.33}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.7}{\ignorespaces Accuracy at coverage: the scorer ranked by its own maximum softmax probability on this test set. Read down a column and accuracy rises as coverage falls; read across and the row-scaling gain is visible. This is a test-set ranking curve, not a deployment abstention guarantee. Pooled over three seeds and the 1000-row test split.\relax }}{47}{table.caption.38}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.8}{\ignorespaces Billed prompt tokens against repetition of one passage on a live token-priced CDN, read from the provider's own \texttt {usage} field --- the exact quantity a per-million-token price multiplies. If a CDN compressed variable-length sequences, the slope would be below one passage.\relax }}{48}{table.caption.40}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.9}{\ignorespaces The four axes, with the evidence class of each cell. ``Measured'' means a script in this repository produced the number from files under \texttt {results/}. ``Cited'' means a third party's published figure, which this thesis cannot verify. No cell is a head-to-head: the two systems were never run on the same rows.\relax }}{49}{table.caption.41}%
